---
import InfoPage from "../layouts/InfoPage.astro";
import CodeCard from "../components/CodeCard.astro";
import site from "../data/site.ts";
import toolsData from "../data/tools.json";

/* Counts and the live-tool list come from tools.json (recalibrate.mjs). The
 * settings mirror docs/configuration.md in the product repo, which is the
 * full reference. */
const coreCount = toolsData.core.length;
const registered = toolsData.toolCount;
const liveTools = toolsData.sections.find((s) => s.label.startsWith("Live org data"))?.tools ?? [];
const bare = (n: string): string => n.replace(/^sfi\./, "");
const docsUrl = `${site.github}/blob/main/docs/configuration.md`;

const title = "sf-intelligence Configuration Reference";
const description =
  "Settings for sf-intelligence: choosing the vault, the default tool set, the optional live mode, network and update checks, HTTP serving and large orgs.";

const sections = [
  { id: "vault", label: "Choosing the vault" },
  { id: "profile", label: "Default tool set" },
  { id: "live", label: "Live mode" },
  { id: "network", label: "Network and updates" },
  { id: "limits", label: "Response size and routing" },
  { id: "http", label: "HTTP serving" },
  { id: "large-orgs", label: "Large orgs" },
  { id: "multi-org", label: "Several orgs" },
  { id: "logs", label: "Logs and overlays" },
];
---

<InfoPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/configuration"
  heading="Configuration"
  lede="Most setups need none of this: the defaults are offline and read-only. Reach for these settings when you want live record facts, several orgs, or more control."
  crumbs={[{ name: "Getting started", path: "/getting-started" }, { name: "Configuration", path: "/configuration" }]}
  meta="For developers"
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    {sections.map((s) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>)}
  </nav>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="vault">
    <h2 id="vault">Choosing the vault</h2>
    <p>Every command that reads or writes a vault, and the MCP server itself, takes <code>--vault &lt;path&gt;</code>: the <code>org-kb</code> folder or the folder that holds it. Precedence is the flag, then <code>SFI_VAULT</code>, then <code>./org-kb</code>. A named path with no vault is an error, never a silent fallback.</p>
    <p>Always pass an absolute path in MCP host config, because hosts start the server from a folder you don’t control. At startup <code>sfi mcp</code> prints the vault it serves and the org it was built from, so a wrong-org session is easy to spot.</p>
    <CodeCard id="cfg-vault" file="Terminal" code={'npx -y sf-intelligence mcp --vault "/full/path/to/your-project/org-kb"'} />
    <p>A vault records its own folder. A vault that was copied or moved refuses writes from commands until you run the one-line fix it prints.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="profile">
    <h2 id="profile">Default tool set</h2>
    <p>By default the server advertises {coreCount} tools ({registered} are registered): the basics plus the tools behind the questions the site advertises. Every other analysis gives the same output through <code>sfi.run_analysis</code>. The profile is fixed when the server starts.</p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_TOOL_PROFILE</code></td><td><code>core</code></td><td><code>core</code>: the {coreCount} default tools. <code>full</code>: advertise and call every tool directly. Unknown values warn and fall back to <code>core</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="meta-line">The list: <a href="/tools#core">Tools reference</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="live">
    <h2 id="live">Live mode</h2>
    <p>Off by default. Snapshot tools never call Salesforce. When on, only a fixed set of read-only live tools can run; there is no free-form SOQL tool.</p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Scope</th><th scope="col">How</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Standing grant</td><td>Per org; lasts 7 days</td><td><code>sfi.live_consent {"{"} grant: true {"}"}</code>. Binds the org id and user through a read-only <code>sf org display</code>. Default scope is aggregate counts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>More scopes</td><td>Same grant</td><td>Grant again with <code>scopes: ["sample"]</code> and/or <code>["users"]</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Environment</td><td>The whole MCP process</td><td><code>SFI_LIVE_PLANE_ENABLED=1</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Revoke with <code>sfi.live_consent {"{"} revoke: true {"}"}</code>. A per-call <code>liveEnabled: true</code> is a request, not consent: it never turns live mode on by itself. Grants are stored in <code>~/.sf-intelligence/live-consent.json</code> (override with <code>SFI_CONSENT_PATH</code>), so they work before a vault exists.</p>

    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_LIVE_QUERY_BUDGET</code></td><td><code>50</code></td><td>Live queries per server session. When it’s spent, live tools stop and say so. Cache hits are free.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_LIVE_CACHE_TTL_MS</code></td><td><code>90000</code></td><td>In-memory cache for repeated live queries, stamped <code>cached: true</code> with the original read time.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_BLAST_RADIUS_MAX_LIVE</code></td><td><code>25</code></td><td>Per-call cap on live counts for <code>sfi.blast_radius_live</code>; beyond it the answer is marked partial.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Some answers combine the snapshot (what depends on what) with a live count (how many records), labelled <code>hybrid</code> with both freshness stamps. If the org is ahead of the snapshot, the answer leads with that warning. Without a grant these tools still answer from the snapshot, with a note.</p>

    <details class="dev-details">
      <summary>The {liveTools.length} live tools</summary>
      <ul>
        {liveTools.map((t) => <li><a href={`/tools#${bare(t.name)}`}><code>{t.name}</code></a> · {t.description}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="network">
    <h2 id="network">Network and updates</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_NETWORK_MODE</code></td><td><code>off</code></td><td>No outbound network from the MCP process. <code>updates-only</code> allows the npm update check; <code>salesforce-read</code> allows retrieve and live reads. <code>refresh</code> and live calls step up only for their own duration.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_UPDATE_CHECK</code></td><td>off</td><td>Set to <code>1</code> to check npm for a newer version at start-up (one GET, about a 3-second timeout, cached a day, off in CI). <code>SFI_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> forces it off.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_TELEMETRY_OPTIN</code> + <code>SFI_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>off</td><td>An opt-in daily counter after a fresh update check. Both must be set; the only payload is the event name and the UTC day.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Separately, with no network at all, <code>sfi.health_check</code> tells you when the vault was built by an older version and should be refreshed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Response size and routing</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES</code></td><td><code>40000</code></td><td>Byte budget per tool response. Lists are trimmed first and the trim is disclosed; an answer that still can’t fit returns an <code>oversize</code> error naming that tool’s narrowing options. Raise only if your client accepts larger results.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_ROUTER_MODE</code></td><td>hybrid</td><td>By default <code>sfi.route_question</code> returns ranked suggestions and the host decides. <code>offline</code> makes its route authoritative, for CI and hosts with no model.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>About 40 analysis tools accept a selector (a component id, an API name, a name fragment or a namespace) and echo back the <code>appliedScope</code> they used. If selectors disagree or none matches, the tool refuses with <code>invalid-query</code> instead of answering org-wide. Leave the selector off and you get the unscoped answer, unchanged.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="http">
    <h2 id="http">HTTP serving</h2>
    <p><code>sfi serve --http</code> serves the same read-only tools over streamable HTTP for CI or shared use on one machine. It needs a bearer token (<code>--generate-token</code> prints one once), binds <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default, and keeps live mode off over HTTP whatever the consent or environment says. For a team server, <code>--tokens-file</code> takes a list of tokens with ids used to attribute annotation writes. stdio serving stays <code>sfi mcp</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="large-orgs">
    <h2 id="large-orgs">Large orgs</h2>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div><dt><code>refresh --staged</code></dt><dd>A usable vault in minutes. A skeleton card first, then the families behind most questions (objects, fields, validation rules, flows, Apex, layouts, record types, profiles, permission sets), then the full refresh, swapped in only if it succeeds. Until a family lands, answers about it stay qualified.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Reports and dashboards</dt><dd>Every full refresh pulls the most-used <code>SFI_REPORTS_CAP</code> (default 500) by last run or view. Above the cap, the rest show as not checked. <code>--with-reports</code> pulls all; <code>--no-reports</code> skips them.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Apex parsing</dt><dd>On by default: a real parser reads field reads, writes and calls (labelled parsed). <code>--no-apex-ast</code> turns it off at the cost of weaker labels.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>refresh --no-pull</code></dt><dd>Rebuilds from the source already on disk without contacting the org.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="multi-org">
    <h2 id="multi-org">Several orgs</h2>
    <p>Cross-org tools (<code>compare_vaults</code>, <code>fleet_drift_ranking</code> and others) read a registry of vault paths. Register each vault with an absolute path:</p>
    <CodeCard id="cfg-fleet" file="Terminal" code={"sfi register-vault prod /full/path/to/prod/org-kb\nsfi register-vault uat /full/path/to/uat/org-kb\nsfi list-vaults"} />
    <p><code>SF_INTELLIGENCE_REGISTRY_PATH</code> points at a <code>registry.json</code> or the folder holding it. Freshness is read from each vault’s own manifest, so the registry never goes stale. Several read-only servers can share one vault; only a refresh needs the exclusive lock.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="logs">
    <h2 id="logs">Logs and overlays</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>SF_INTELLIGENCE_AUDIT_LOG</code></td><td>off</td><td>Append-only JSONL: tool name, argument <em>keys</em> (never values), vault hash, time.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SFI_METRICS_LOG</code></td><td>off</td><td>Per-call JSONL: tool, ok or error, duration, payload size. No arguments or org content.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>~/.sf-intelligence/question-gaps.jsonl</code></td><td>local file</td><td>Questions the router couldn’t place, kept locally and never sent anywhere.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>meta/annotations.jsonl</code></td><td>in the vault</td><td>Owner, status, synonyms and notes that survive refreshes. People write with <code>sfi annotate</code>; an assistant can only propose.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sfi vault git enable</code></td><td>off</td><td>Turns the vault into its own git repo; refreshes that change the source commit a summary.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="full">
    <h2 id="full">The full reference</h2>
    <p>This page covers the settings people use. Everything else, including development and release-gate variables, is in <a href={docsUrl} rel="noopener">docs/configuration.md</a> in the repository.</p>
    <ul class="link-list">
      <li><a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a></li>
      <li><a href="/trust">Trust</a><span> · what’s guaranteed and why</span></li>
      <li><a href="/tools">Tools reference</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</InfoPage>
